<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Satyashodhak Samaj</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1873</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Truth-seekers' Society</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Satyashodhak Samaj, the Truth-seekers' Society, was a social reform movement founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> in 1873 by Jyotirao Phule. It campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> discrimination and the religious and social dominance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, and for the education of lower castes and women. In the early 20th century it was strongly supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahu-of-kolhapur">Shahu of Kolhapur</a> and fed the non-Brahmin movement in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/satyashodhak-samaj.jpg" alt="Satyashodhak Samaj" width="250" height="354" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mphule.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
